WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.583
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.000
- Well, welcome back to another
episode of the "Addy Hour".

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.740
Again, I'm honored to be able
to host these conversations

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.600
and stay really honored again,

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.110
and excited to be able to
jump into a conversation topic

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.090
that we haven't addressed
up to this point,

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.860
and that's really thinking
about mental health

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.120
in the military.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.190
So today I'm honored to be
able to host three guests

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.810
for a conversation about mental health,

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:29.350
the military, and the
unseen wounds of war.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:32.490
The first guest I'd like to
introduce is Steven Elliott.

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:35.390
Steven is a veteran of
the war in Afghanistan,

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:38.450
having served there as
an army ranger in 2004.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:41.220
He's also the author of
"War Story", a memoir,

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.580
in which he details his journey
to and from the battlefield,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:48.040
and the reality of the
unseen wounds of war.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.130
It's a really great book,

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:51.090
very compelling story
and important narrative,

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:51.923
which I've read.

00:00:51.923 --> 00:00:52.920
So I encourage you to check it out

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.130
if you haven't read it already.

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:56.380
Also did want as mention Steven

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:57.450
as someone who has been featured

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:02.077
on ESPN, NPR, NBC Fox
News, MSNBC, and others,

00:01:02.077 --> 00:01:04.840
and the hope that he
and his family's story

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.030
would help others also
find hope and healing.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:09.670
He's someone who's a native of Kansas,

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:11.160
he's currently an entrepreneur

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.900
and resides in Southern California

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.860
with his wife and two daughters.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.090
And someone I was able to
connect with a few years back,

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:18.990
glad that we have this
opportunity to reconnect.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.730
I wanna make sure I
welcome Stephen Elliott

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:22.900
to the "Addy Hour" podcast.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.060
- Thanks for having me.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:24.893
- Of course.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:27.890
The next guest I'd like to introduce

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:29.963
is Ellsworth "Tony" Williams.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.710
Tony is someone who is
a combat army veteran,

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:36.480
having faithfully and
honorably served an active duty

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.400
in the army for 24
years, eight and listed,

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.140
and 16 years commissioned.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.260
This includes service during Desert Storm,

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.170
Operation Iraqi Freedom and
Operation Enduring Freedom.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.117
He's also someone who's
received numerous awards

00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:50.830
including a Bronze Star

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:53.750
and three Meritorious Service Medals.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:55.570
One thing that I really admire about Tony

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.750
is that after he retired in 2007,

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.210
he actually decided to rebrand himself

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:01.630
and get a master's degree

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:05.320
in Clinical Mental Health
Counseling from Troy University.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.370
Upon graduation, he founded
and is currently the president

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.290
and CEO of Veterans Counseling Veterans,

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:14.030
and he continues in this
really important work.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:15.260
I will mention this on the side,

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.310
I watched one of the
recent updates that he did,

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.550
and I was really encouraged by the energy

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:22.030
that he brought as he was
dancing to Pharrell's "Happy"

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.960
at the beginning of that update,

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.110
and I thought that was great

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.820
just to be able to lead off in that way.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.870
Wanna make sure I welcome
"Tony" Williams here

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:29.890
to the "Addy Hour" podcast.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:30.990
Thanks for being here.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.770
And the third guest who
I'd like to introduce

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:39.350
is Dr. Barbara Rothbaum,
who is the director

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.450
of the Emory Healthcare Veterans Program,

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:42.680
and also the director

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:46.320
of the Trauma and Anxiety
Recovery Program at Emory.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:47.229
She holds numerous titles

00:02:47.229 --> 00:02:50.340
and has lots of honorary titles as well,

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.360
but I did just want to
mention some of her work

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.320
that she has also been involved in.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.860
Dr. Rothbaum is someone
who specializes in research

00:02:56.860 --> 00:03:01.230
on the treatment of anxiety
disorders, particular PTSD.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.164
She's also been able to contribute
to lots of conversations

00:03:04.164 --> 00:03:08.430
and to reports around policy as well,

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:10.240
and has briefed the Department of Defense,

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.470
the Veterans Association
or Veterans Affairs,

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.910
the House and Senate
Committees on Veterans' Affairs

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:16.560
and the Armed Services Committees

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.700
on the Institute of Medicines report

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:20.820
around some of these topics.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:23.330
Dr. Rothbaum is someone who's
been studying PTSD treatments

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:26.500
since 1986, and she has also developed,

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:29.270
tested and disseminated
some of the most innovative

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.470
and effective treatments
available for PTSD,

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:33.870
which I'm sure we'll talk about today.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.300
She's also authored
numerous scientific papers,

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:39.360
has also published over 11 books.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.327
The most recent of which is entitled

00:03:41.327 --> 00:03:43.520
"PTSD for the General Public:

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.890
PTSD What Everyone Needs to Know".

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:49.220
So again, someone who has
a long standing history

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.260
and effort and effectiveness in this work,

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:52.630
and we're grateful to be
able to welcome Barbara

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:54.700
to the program today as well.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:56.060
- Thank you for having me.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:57.610
- Of course.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:59.020
So as my listeners know,

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:01.900
I always just start out
checking in with everybody

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:03.920
to see how everyone is
doing at this moment

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:07.320
as we are at the end of February of 2022.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.390
And with everything that
we continue to experience,

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:10.290
I think it's just helpful

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:12.640
to kind of center our conversation there

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:13.710
as a launching point.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.610
So I'm really gonna reverse
order of our introductions

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:18.210
and actually start with Barbara.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:20.960
- I'm very excited.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:23.380
So we've pivoted back and forth

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.870
with our veteran program over COVID.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:29.080
With the beginning, we went telemedicine,

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:34.080
we came back with the omicron
variant and the surge.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:36.450
In January, we went
telemedicine (indistinct).

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:39.790
Again, this week we've brought
people back in person again,

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:43.930
so it's just exciting to
have veterans on site again.

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:44.910
- Well, that's wonderful to hear,

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:46.640
I know that's been quite a journey.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.450
And just from what I've seen

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:49.680
from some of the work you all done,

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:51.360
seems that you pivoted pretty well

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.510
in terms of really
getting the message across

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.190
to let people know the
surges were available

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:56.320
and still highly effective,

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.620
but I'm sure it's exciting
to come back in person.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:04:59.470
- Yeah, no, they are.

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:01.790
And we gather data on everything we do,

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:05.280
and we're so thrilled that
our telemedicine treatment

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.769
is just as effective, but it's
nice to have people on site.

00:05:08.769 --> 00:05:11.970
- Yeah, yeah, definitely, I agree.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:12.803
Tony, how about you?

00:05:12.803 --> 00:05:14.130
How are you doing these days?

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:14.963
No, I wasn't,

00:05:14.963 --> 00:05:16.590
I don't know if you're gonna
dance for us as well, but

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.180
(Addy chuckles)

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:20.920
I think you're on mute.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.240
- So where's the music playing?

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.503
So, I tell you, one thing about,

00:05:23.503 --> 00:05:28.503
'cause I'm basically introverted
and this COVID forced me

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:30.804
to become more extroverted
with my virtuals.

00:05:30.804 --> 00:05:33.250
So it wasn't for that, you
would not have seen me on.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:35.600
So most of the stuff I've done,
which have been in person,

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.050
which really is only people
who are there know it exists.

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:40.070
And because of the
amicron and stuff going,

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:41.060
and all that stuffs going on

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:43.500
and it forced to do all
my events virtually,

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.610
which now has increased
people that see what I do.

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:48.650
So that, and one side of it,

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:51.087
I've seen some positive as far
as that reaching more people

00:05:51.087 --> 00:05:52.100
and some of that.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:55.680
And accessible, as a nonprofit
is definitely more economical

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.990
to do it virtual versus these
events that we're going in.

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:00.750
So that's a good thing here.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:03.540
I'm still trying to monitor how COVID,

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:05.530
and I'm hopefully, these guests
can talk about a little bit,

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:07.237
is how has COVID affected suicide?

00:06:07.237 --> 00:06:09.510
You know, that's my name, bread and butter

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:10.427
when it comes to the
military and veterans,

00:06:10.427 --> 00:06:12.310
'cause you know,

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:14.423
I have, everything I know is anecdotal,

00:06:15.486 --> 00:06:17.000
and if you really see those,
so many reports to come out

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:18.490
from the DOD, you've
seen something different.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:21.223
So I'm definitely curious to
hear from these guests here,

00:06:21.223 --> 00:06:22.960
have they seen any kind of differences

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:25.990
when it comes to COVID
in the veteran space?

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:27.130
- Yeah, that's definitely a topic

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:29.270
we should definitely make
sure we loop back to.

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:31.610
Encouraged to hear that
in a way the silver lines

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:33.740
that you've been able to expand your reach

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:35.500
in some ways as well, so.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.070
I applaud for taking advantage of that

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:38.920
and being flexible, again,

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:41.750
just in the moment that
we're in as well, so.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:44.103
Steven, how about you, how
are you doing these days?

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:46.210
- Doing okay.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.850
We had the last couple
years have been certainly

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:51.100
what they've been for all of our society.

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:53.780
And we decided to throw in a move

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.660
from the Seattle area
of Southern California,

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.747
just 'cause we thought
that'd be fun (indistinct).

00:06:59.212 --> 00:07:01.248
And so yeah, it's been a weird,

00:07:01.248 --> 00:07:05.370
the last couple years have been, you know,

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:07.560
the problems that we've had
to deal with in our household,

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:08.980
you know, I'll take those any day

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:10.820
over the ones that a lot of
people had to struggle with.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:15.370
So I found myself much more inward focused

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:16.460
than I would like to be,

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:19.080
just in terms of navigating a move

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.180
and navigating changes and
enterprises I'm engaged with

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:26.360
that are kind of resolving
and yeah, really grateful.

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:29.206
I mean, our household is healthy

00:07:29.206 --> 00:07:32.420
and we just feel really fortunate

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:34.670
to be where we're at and, yeah.

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:36.800
And I don't know if
excited is the right word,

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:40.330
but certainly, ready to,
whatever extent we can,

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.220
continue to reengage in this conversation.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:45.050
So yeah, happy to be here.

00:07:45.050 --> 00:07:46.020
- Excellent.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:48.960
Again, sounds like a lot
of flexibility and some,

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:50.580
I don't know what the right word is,

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.420
bravery maybe, on your part,
moving it in the middle,

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:54.553
but it sounds like things are.

00:07:54.553 --> 00:07:55.560
(Addy chuckles)

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:56.570
- It worked out.

00:07:56.570 --> 00:07:59.627
We started the process prior to COVID

00:07:59.627 --> 00:08:01.210
and I continued with that process

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:03.900
and yeah, again, it's, you know, you know,

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.320
moving is what it is,

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:10.080
but that's self-imposed and
we're on the other side of it

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:10.940
and (indistinct).
- Right, right.

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:12.440
- Glad to be here, so.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:13.897
- Yeah, that's wonderful to hear.

00:08:13.897 --> 00:08:15.160
Now I appreciate it, in the way

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:16.350
that you put everything in context

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:19.440
to even just acknowledging
what challenges you have had,

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:21.650
but also knowing that there
are lots of other things

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:23.387
that a lot of people have
gone through to be clear.

00:08:23.387 --> 00:08:25.550
And I think that ties
into a lot of the stories

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:28.370
and the work that you all do as well.

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:31.960
So before we kinda jump
into some of the the meat

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:33.070
of the conversation, so to speak,

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:34.603
I also wanted to give you all a chance

00:08:34.603 --> 00:08:37.040
just to talk about the
work and the initiatives

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:38.690
that you're involved in.

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:39.523
So Steven, actually,

00:08:39.523 --> 00:08:40.900
if you could start out just
telling us a little bit

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:41.840
about the Elliott Funds

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:44.960
and what you all do as an organization.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:47.710
- Yeah, the Elliott Fund
was originally established

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:50.210
in conjunction with the
publishing of "War Story"

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:51.390
a few years ago.

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.590
Really, there wasn't,

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:55.577
I'd like to say there was
a lot of strategic thought

00:08:55.577 --> 00:08:58.040
put into it, there wasn't.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:01.220
It was simply established
to be a pass through entity

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:03.400
for any book proceeds,

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:05.360
'cause that was one of
the things that, for us,

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:06.193
we felt it was important,

00:09:06.193 --> 00:09:08.470
was that if there was
any financial resources

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:09.850
that that book produced,

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:12.440
we wanted to be able to channel
those to other nonprofits

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:14.660
that were doing good work
in the veteran space.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:17.420
So really, that was, that's
all it was initially,

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:22.110
and then, we've continued to
kind of use that as a platform,

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:24.300
really, of connection between resources

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:25.960
and other organizations.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:29.780
And right now the main
work that we're doing,

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:32.590
we, being my wife and I, my wife, Brooke,

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:34.490
is we're working closely

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:37.730
with a film producer named Beth Dolan,

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:41.380
who's in the finishing
stages of post-production

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:45.290
for a documentary film
called "Stranger at Home"

00:09:45.290 --> 00:09:47.320
that really chronicles the,

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:48.300
it chronicles a story

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:51.510
of a Military Mental Health Whistleblower

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:54.230
named Dr. Mark Russell, who's
a former Naval Commander

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:59.220
and who, in the early stages,
you know, pre-Iraq invasion,

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:03.420
was adamantly raising
concerns as to the gaps

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:06.690
between the DOD state of
policy for mental health

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:10.620
and the actual practical gaps
for how that was applied.

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:13.890
And so that documentary which we've seen,

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:18.280
the rough cut of it's pretty compelling.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:21.310
We'll be using whatever
resources we have in our platform

00:10:23.244 --> 00:10:24.980
to advocate both for that film

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:29.980
and for policy changes within
the active duty component

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:33.810
to help curtail the military
mental health epidemic.

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:36.400
So that's kinda what
we're up to right now.

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:37.450
- That's really encouraging to hear

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:39.530
that you've been able to
continue in those efforts

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:41.210
and some of these important topics.

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:42.520
I'll be curious as we go through,

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:45.280
even with some of Dr Barbara's experiences

00:10:45.280 --> 00:10:46.780
to maybe even comment
on some of the things

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:49.940
that she's seen in her
perspective as well.

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:50.840
Before we get there, though,

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:52.360
Tony, I wanted to give you also a chance

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:55.370
to talk a little bit about
Veterans Counseling Veterans

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:58.073
on the heels of what Steven
has just mentioned as well.

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:02.350
- So I founded the Veterans
Council Veterans in 2014

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:05.140
and as I, actually,
after I intern at the VA

00:11:06.264 --> 00:11:08.253
and what people don't
understand and don't know,

00:11:08.253 --> 00:11:09.540
it's not a slide on them,

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:13.500
but 90% of the providers aren't veterans.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.470
And I realized that
when I was getting the,

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:17.630
my interns and talking to them.

00:11:17.630 --> 00:11:19.460
So that's why I came with
the idea of veterans,

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:21.590
why can't veterans council veterans.

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:23.790
And it's a Veterans Council
Veterans is a nonprofit

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:25.475
that consists of veterans
and spouse of veterans

00:11:25.475 --> 00:11:26.620
who are in a mental profession.

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:28.320
Doesn't matter, it's almost like a,

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:30.770
it doesn't matter if you're
clinical psychologist

00:11:31.892 --> 00:11:35.390
or LCSW, (indistinct).

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:36.687
And so I just wanna
get away from identity,

00:11:36.687 --> 00:11:39.890
we try to get away from identity
of being a social worker

00:11:39.890 --> 00:11:41.260
and being more of a veteran is helpful

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:43.890
versus I'm a "social worker",
or I'm a "psychologist".

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:45.680
That's, to me, is what breaking, we got,

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:47.020
I wanted to break through.

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:48.750
Veteran Council Veterans,
its prior mission

00:11:48.750 --> 00:11:51.740
is suicide prevention,
veteran suicide prevention.

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:54.040
And we do this with the
model we call TEAMS,

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:56.810
is teaching education,
advocacy, mentorship,

00:11:56.810 --> 00:11:59.300
and services, and many arenas.

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:01.620
And so, and we try to take the risk factor

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:03.730
and turn it to protective facet.

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:05.110
One of the things that
is different from us

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:07.020
is that people don't understand that,

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:09.610
don't realize that over
about 7% of the military,

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:13.310
of the veterans, have a
relationship, one way or the, okay?

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:15.950
So we, you know, you lose that,
when you're in the service,

00:12:15.950 --> 00:12:17.990
they get as much service as you get,

00:12:17.990 --> 00:12:19.043
but once you get out of the service,

00:12:19.043 --> 00:12:21.140
it's just a veteran that
gets all the services.

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:23.620
And so I wanted to make
sure I include them

00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:24.780
in everything I do when it comes to that.

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:26.580
So we focus, not just on the veteran,

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:28.210
we also focus on the family as well,

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:30.150
so is in the family, so is in the veteran.

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:32.810
So those are the things we
have and we come up next week,

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:36.100
we're doing a Veterans' Family
Mental Wellness Workshop

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:37.320
for the veterans' families.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:38.920
We're gonna do a Military
Psycho-trauma Workshop,

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:40.230
and we're gonna have a summit as well.

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:43.100
Military Veterans, Suicide
Veteran Summit, our fourth one,

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:45.910
and it's gonna have faith,
peers, mental health,

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:48.250
and the community, you need all four

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:49.540
to have any kind of (indistinct).

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:51.780
Veterans suicide is a national problem,

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:53.520
but it has a local solution.

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:55.439
You cannot nationalize it,
you can't franchise it,

00:12:55.439 --> 00:12:56.490
and you can't monopolize it.

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:58.890
You have to look at the local
community to really fix it.

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:00.450
You can't take something, like right now,

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:02.250
one of my biggest concerns

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:04.770
is that whenever you have a
crisis, you call this number,

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:07.600
it's a VA number, but
they're all in Kannadiga.

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:09.230
Okay, why do I gotta call Kannadiga?

00:13:09.230 --> 00:13:11.200
My house is on fire down the street,

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:12.957
why do I call on the fire
department in New York

00:13:12.957 --> 00:13:15.000
to put my house out?

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:17.520
So we gotta get away from that,

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.130
that's the thing I'm
trying to push as much

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:21.750
we gotta start involving
more of the community

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:23.460
when it comes to suicide prevention,

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:24.293
intervention, and postvention.

00:13:24.293 --> 00:13:25.820
So that's my thing.

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:26.910
Thanks, (indistinct).

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:28.780
