You never know who's
gonna take you anywhere,

00:21:53.160 --> 00:21:55.210
so being hypervigilant, looking,
who's that by the corner,

00:21:55.210 --> 00:21:56.430
hey, you looking at me (indistinct),

00:21:56.430 --> 00:21:59.140
that's part of what it
takes to survive over there,

00:21:59.140 --> 00:22:01.870
that you cannot use the
same things you learned

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:04.490
that kept you alive over there, over here.

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:07.330
So I'm just saying that we gotta look at,

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:09.816
and I haven't even talk
about the families,

00:22:09.816 --> 00:22:10.820
is a whole done dynamic,

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:14.901
but as an individual, we cannot
keep using the same model

00:22:14.901 --> 00:22:18.510
of an individual, and then
trying to think it works for us,

00:22:18.510 --> 00:22:19.640
we weren't trained that way.

00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:23.080
We were trained like
the Borg on "Star Trek",

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:25.924
we were trained as the
collectors, the collective,

00:22:25.924 --> 00:22:28.120
and that's what we were used
to trying to get part of,

00:22:28.120 --> 00:22:30.400
that collectiveness and
not that individual,

00:22:30.400 --> 00:22:31.233
I wanna be rich.

00:22:31.233 --> 00:22:32.900
So I just thought I wanted to relay that

00:22:32.900 --> 00:22:35.410
to when Steve was talking
about beating at the bar

00:22:35.410 --> 00:22:38.310
or some of the most effective
people, through a suicide,

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:39.920
is not somebody with a PhD

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:41.400
or 3000 letters on the end of the lane,

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:43.537
but it's a regular Joe
that sit through and say,

00:22:43.537 --> 00:22:45.390
"Hey man, let's talk."

00:22:45.390 --> 00:22:46.500
- Yeah.

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:49.310
- So anyway, that's my
spill with my comment

00:22:49.310 --> 00:22:51.170
when it comes to suicide.

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:52.297
- Yeah, really well said.

00:22:52.297 --> 00:22:55.340
And I appreciate you
pulling out those nuances

00:22:55.340 --> 00:22:56.490
for us as well.

00:22:56.490 --> 00:22:58.350
I'm curious for the other two as well,

00:22:58.350 --> 00:22:59.770
like how you would react to that,

00:22:59.770 --> 00:23:02.120
because it seems that
there's also this tension

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:03.340
between the individual,

00:23:03.340 --> 00:23:05.220
but you've also all
alluded to the importance

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:07.890
of the relationships as
well, and their family.

00:23:07.890 --> 00:23:10.480
So how have you seen your experience?

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:13.210
How do people walk
through that practically,

00:23:13.210 --> 00:23:15.340
still try and get the attention
they need individually,

00:23:15.340 --> 00:23:17.533
but now within the context
of other relationships,

00:23:17.533 --> 00:23:20.910
not necessarily in the context
of a military relationship?

00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:24.580
Any insights or experiences
you all wanna share?

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:26.488
- I can talk for four days about it, yeah.

00:23:26.488 --> 00:23:27.321
(Addy and Barbara chuckles)

00:23:27.321 --> 00:23:29.040
I'll try not to,

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:32.840
and I'll focus in my
comments right now on PTSD,

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:35.200
but I know we're not
just talking about PTSD.

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:40.200
And I think we tend to believe our stories

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:43.770
and if it's something that's associated

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:45.967
with a traumatic event and
there's a lot of avoidance,

00:23:45.967 --> 00:23:47.650
and we don't even wanna think about it,

00:23:47.650 --> 00:23:48.620
we don't wanna talk about it,

00:23:48.620 --> 00:23:50.910
we don't want anything to remind us of it,

00:23:50.910 --> 00:23:53.610
then we can't possibly
think differently about it.

00:23:53.610 --> 00:23:57.810
And so what we do is help
people confront those memories,

00:23:57.810 --> 00:24:01.210
confront the triggers and
do it in a therapeutic way

00:24:01.210 --> 00:24:03.480
that then they can take it
out and they can look at it.

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:06.790
So they might be thinking what happened,

00:24:06.790 --> 00:24:09.290
I was, you know, Jones got killed,

00:24:09.290 --> 00:24:12.400
it was my fault 'cause I was
driving when we hit the IED.

00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:13.410
And if you can't go there,

00:24:13.410 --> 00:24:15.040
that's what they're carrying with them.

00:24:15.040 --> 00:24:16.610
When we work on it,

00:24:16.610 --> 00:24:18.660
they might be able to
change how they think,

00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:21.450
that it sucks that Jones got killed,

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:22.460
but as we went through it,

00:24:22.460 --> 00:24:25.130
I did everything I could, there
were eight eyes on the road,

00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:28.850
it was the insurgent planning that IED.

00:24:28.850 --> 00:24:32.800
And so once we can help work them

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:36.080
to have a different perception
of themselves in the world,

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:38.290
I mean, it's kind of what
you guys were saying too,

00:24:38.290 --> 00:24:41.310
then that, we translate it completely

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:45.100
by involving the family,
by involving other people

00:24:45.100 --> 00:24:47.610
to help work on their
relationships as well.

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:49.450
So it's, you know,

00:24:49.450 --> 00:24:50.860
I guess it's a chicken and an egg

00:24:50.860 --> 00:24:53.390
and you start wherever you start.

00:24:53.390 --> 00:24:56.370
So a lot of times we'll start
with the individual work

00:24:56.370 --> 00:25:00.340
and helping people see it more clearly,

00:25:00.340 --> 00:25:02.130
and then I think that can help

00:25:02.130 --> 00:25:04.610
with some of the relationships.

00:25:04.610 --> 00:25:06.568
- So it sounds like stepwise
in that sense as well.

00:25:06.568 --> 00:25:07.401
- Yeah.

00:25:07.401 --> 00:25:09.213
- Steven, I'd be curious
in your own experience too,

00:25:09.213 --> 00:25:12.181
'cause you alluded to avoiding
the hope in that sense

00:25:12.181 --> 00:25:15.410
and because of possible disappointment,

00:25:15.410 --> 00:25:17.990
is there a way that you can
recall that you were able

00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:19.300
to make that transition?

00:25:19.300 --> 00:25:21.670
Was it also similarly focused
on you as an individual

00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:24.760
or were there other processes or coaches

00:25:24.760 --> 00:25:26.910
that were helpful in your situation?

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:27.743
- Well, I mean, for me,

00:25:27.743 --> 00:25:30.480
it was just a question of
like how bad it'd have to get

00:25:30.480 --> 00:25:32.260
before he wanted to get help.

00:25:32.260 --> 00:25:35.660
And for me it took the
point at which, you know,

00:25:35.660 --> 00:25:37.480
my wife knew a lot more what was going on

00:25:37.480 --> 00:25:38.453
with me than I did,

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:42.660
and it was a point at which, you know,

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:46.180
after X number of years
saying, I wanna divorce.

00:25:46.180 --> 00:25:48.560
And then is at that point which,

00:25:48.560 --> 00:25:51.740
that was 13 years ago and we're married

00:25:51.740 --> 00:25:55.710
and that's a really
blessed part of the story,

00:25:55.710 --> 00:25:58.530
but at a certain point you
just sort of find yourself

00:25:58.530 --> 00:26:01.800
sitting in a place that
you never intended to go,

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:04.320
at no point did you
look at the map and say,

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:07.040
yeah, I wanna go to moving out

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:09.490
and living with two other single guys

00:26:09.490 --> 00:26:11.610
after having been married
and having a house,

00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:13.300
that kind of feels like a step backwards,

00:26:13.300 --> 00:26:14.420
no offense to that,

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:16.550
but that was not where I was trying to go.

00:26:16.550 --> 00:26:19.460
And then, it took a pretty,

00:26:19.460 --> 00:26:23.900
yeah, it took a pretty thick
metaphorical two by four for me

00:26:23.900 --> 00:26:26.740
to sort of wake up and realize,

00:26:26.740 --> 00:26:30.970
maybe if I keep doing or
not doing the same thing,

00:26:30.970 --> 00:26:33.350
I'm gonna keep getting the same thing.

00:26:33.350 --> 00:26:35.520
And if that's what I want then fine,

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:38.160
but if I want a different result in life,

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:41.840
then maybe I'm gonna have
to try something else.

00:26:41.840 --> 00:26:44.790
And I didn't have, I
didn't have a, you know,

00:26:44.790 --> 00:26:49.600
for me it was partly a, it
was an absence of context

00:26:50.480 --> 00:26:54.300
because I grew up around
a lot of veterans.

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:57.037
My grandfather was a
veteran of World War II,

00:26:57.037 --> 00:26:58.890
fought in Italy for a year and a half.

00:26:58.890 --> 00:27:01.060
And it wasn't until I was an adult

00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:06.060
that I saw the other side of the heroic,

00:27:06.130 --> 00:27:08.790
freeing of Europe from the Germans

00:27:08.790 --> 00:27:10.860
and realized that war is hell.

00:27:10.860 --> 00:27:12.143
And that was something
that he carried with him

00:27:12.143 --> 00:27:16.020
through his life, and he
had a community around him.

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:19.910
But for me, like post-traumatic
stress disorder was,

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:22.670
that was a scary acronym.

00:27:22.670 --> 00:27:23.680
I didn't know what it meant

00:27:23.680 --> 00:27:28.440
other than it meant that I must be weak

00:27:28.440 --> 00:27:30.270
to be experiencing this.

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:32.500
And that's difficult

00:27:32.500 --> 00:27:35.930
when you've come from
a military experience,

00:27:35.930 --> 00:27:39.620
which I think is true
for anybody who joins.

00:27:39.620 --> 00:27:42.000
I don't care if you're
a clerk in the air force

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:46.000
or if you're an army ranger
or anything in between,

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:47.580
you join to serve,

00:27:47.580 --> 00:27:49.830
you want to operate from
a place of strength,

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:56.010
and you have to be willing
to do difficult things

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:58.500
that some other people
just aren't willing to do.

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:02.480
And so then when you
find yourself in a place

00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:05.180
and you know, certainly, the
community that I served in,

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:07.550
that community self-selects poor people

00:28:07.550 --> 00:28:09.570
who just put up with a lot of pain,

00:28:09.570 --> 00:28:10.820
that's really what it is.

00:28:10.820 --> 00:28:12.530
I mean, it's bizarre,

00:28:12.530 --> 00:28:15.067
it's an all-volunteer force
that we have right now,

00:28:15.067 --> 00:28:16.620
and that was an all-volunteer unit.

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:18.220
The only reason you're
there getting yelled at

00:28:18.220 --> 00:28:19.590
and getting put through the pace

00:28:19.590 --> 00:28:21.610
is 'cause you choose to be there.

00:28:21.610 --> 00:28:24.710
And so then having been
self-selected essentially

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:27.950
for compartmentalizing and focusing

00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:30.350
and just accomplishing the task.

00:28:30.350 --> 00:28:33.050
I'm not sure whether it's you,
Dr. Rothbaum, who said it,

00:28:33.050 --> 00:28:36.270
but essentially then it's that
deprogramming piece to say,

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:37.830
hey, it doesn't make any less tough

00:28:37.830 --> 00:28:39.700
to acknowledge the fact that you're hurt

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:42.190
and to acknowledge the fact that,

00:28:42.190 --> 00:28:43.390
hey, it's, you know, we don't,

00:28:43.390 --> 00:28:44.520
there's no stigma around that

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:45.850
with respect to of physical wounds.

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:47.921
If I get shot, nobody's standing around me

00:28:47.921 --> 00:28:50.723
on the battlefield, berating
me for getting shot,

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:53.130
or making me feel guilty.

00:28:53.130 --> 00:28:55.880
Even if I did something stupid,
they're gonna care for me.

00:28:55.880 --> 00:29:00.807
And so that was certainly
a big part of my journey

00:29:01.650 --> 00:29:03.093
within my family.

00:29:04.230 --> 00:29:05.890
It was essentially how much,

00:29:05.890 --> 00:29:07.660
how bad do I really want this to get?

00:29:07.660 --> 00:29:09.700
'Cause it's not going in a good direction.

00:29:09.700 --> 00:29:13.180
And is, am I willing to risk?

00:29:13.180 --> 00:29:16.290
Which in retrospect,
the risk was, you know,

00:29:16.290 --> 00:29:18.820
you see it as this big, but it's really,

00:29:18.820 --> 00:29:20.228
it's a lot of people who wanna help you.

00:29:20.228 --> 00:29:21.110
(Elliott chuckles)

00:29:21.110 --> 00:29:26.040
And it's so much bigger in your mind

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:27.730
than it is in actuality.

00:29:27.730 --> 00:29:31.130
So for me, part of that journey
was just having a language

00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:35.960
and a context to not embrace the trauma

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:38.290
and have that become who I am,

00:29:38.290 --> 00:29:41.170
where then I am, that's
my sole identifier,

00:29:41.170 --> 00:29:44.417
is I'm a wounded veteran of
PTSD and that's who I am,

00:29:44.417 --> 00:29:46.510
that's my business card or something.

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:47.410
But it's to say that,

00:29:47.410 --> 00:29:50.380
no, that's integrated
into part of my experience

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:53.760
and I can actually
experience growth from that,

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:56.170
and I don't have to be
stuck in that place.

00:29:56.170 --> 00:29:59.130
And, you know, that growth
takes a lifetime maybe,

00:29:59.130 --> 00:30:00.833
but that's okay.

00:30:02.210 --> 00:30:04.360
- Yeah, that's really well
said and really powerful,

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:06.566
and appreciated just
your honesty and candor

00:30:06.566 --> 00:30:08.120
in that as well.

00:30:08.120 --> 00:30:10.270
One thing, I mean, I have so
many things that came to mind,

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:11.513
one thing that did come to mind,

00:30:11.513 --> 00:30:13.990
which I'll have you and the
others response to as well,

00:30:13.990 --> 00:30:18.850
is just this, I guess the
aspect of choice and volition,

00:30:18.850 --> 00:30:22.100
which is a whole big, that's
a whole another topic.

00:30:22.100 --> 00:30:23.560
Part of the reason I
bring that up, though, is,

00:30:23.560 --> 00:30:25.360
thinking as a neuroscientist too,

00:30:25.360 --> 00:30:28.130
and in terms of what we know
about what happens to the brain

00:30:28.130 --> 00:30:30.910
during certain situations
and how it can change things,

00:30:30.910 --> 00:30:32.720
some people could get
stuck in a certain mindset.

00:30:32.720 --> 00:30:35.310
And then also thinking about
some of the different episodes

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:36.143
that we've had,

00:30:36.143 --> 00:30:39.610
particularly talking
about mental health needs

00:30:39.610 --> 00:30:41.370
and youth and things like that,

00:30:41.370 --> 00:30:44.180
and some of the advice some
our clinical psychology experts

00:30:44.180 --> 00:30:45.980
have talked about, just
being there for people,

00:30:45.980 --> 00:30:48.130
but not pushing people to
say you have to get help,

00:30:48.130 --> 00:30:51.300
but knowing that you are
there to support them.

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:52.470
So I'm curious with, Steven,

00:30:52.470 --> 00:30:53.750
with everything that you've mentioned

00:30:53.750 --> 00:30:56.220
and the others as well, how people,

00:30:56.220 --> 00:30:58.380
especially in military
circles and veteran circles,

00:30:58.380 --> 00:31:02.280
trying to navigate that
aspect of seeing someone

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:04.470
who you want to be able
to help to move forward,

00:31:04.470 --> 00:31:06.580
but then also knowing that,
Steven, as you mentioned,

00:31:06.580 --> 00:31:08.850
you had to get to a
place where, in extent,

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:11.780
you desired to move forward
and it got bad enough,

00:31:11.780 --> 00:31:13.277
the metaphorical two by four to say,

00:31:13.277 --> 00:31:14.600
"Okay, this isn't a situation

00:31:14.600 --> 00:31:16.640
that I want to go down the road

00:31:16.640 --> 00:31:18.440
and just stay in that place."

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:19.273
And the same time,

00:31:19.273 --> 00:31:21.410
and this is probably my
neuroscience frame we're coming to,

00:31:21.410 --> 00:31:24.130
knowing that there's so much
that happens in the brain

00:31:24.130 --> 00:31:26.970
that keeps certain
patterns happening as well.

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:28.760
So I don't know if I'm
forming this question well,

00:31:28.760 --> 00:31:33.370
but just trying to get a
sense of how you navigate that

00:31:33.370 --> 00:31:34.470
in your own life,

00:31:34.470 --> 00:31:37.750
and in the lives of those
that you love as well,

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:39.480
not push people into treatment per se,

00:31:39.480 --> 00:31:42.620
but also encourage and try
and make that come about.

00:31:42.620 --> 00:31:45.270
So you all are probably gonna
articulate that a lot better

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:46.410
than I just asked the question,

00:31:46.410 --> 00:31:49.340
but just thought that
thought that came to mind.

00:31:49.340 --> 00:31:51.680
- Well, imagine we'll
have different answers.

00:31:51.680 --> 00:31:55.750
So a lot of times, and our
whole program is set up

00:31:55.750 --> 00:31:57.430
to try to break down the barriers to care.

