00:44:44.523 --> 00:44:46.980
in what you're saying
too, as one of the other.

00:44:46.980 --> 00:44:49.470
And as Tony mentioned,
sometimes for a good reason,

00:44:49.470 --> 00:44:50.303
I think as you mentioned as well,

00:44:50.303 --> 00:44:53.780
but times also for poor
and detrimental reasons.

00:44:53.780 --> 00:44:54.613
So I appreciate the way

00:44:54.613 --> 00:44:57.040
you all have pulled those things out.

00:44:57.040 --> 00:44:58.270
Our time is going quickly,

00:44:58.270 --> 00:45:00.010
as we probably go a whole nother hour,

00:45:00.010 --> 00:45:00.887
but I did wanna give a chance,

00:45:00.887 --> 00:45:03.040
and Steven, you've already
started to go there already,

00:45:03.040 --> 00:45:05.610
just to give you all time
to actually talk about,

00:45:05.610 --> 00:45:07.580
how do we move things forward?

00:45:07.580 --> 00:45:09.820
Barbara, I know you've done a lot of work,

00:45:09.820 --> 00:45:11.553
some reports and testimonies
and things like that too.

00:45:11.553 --> 00:45:14.510
There's policy, there's
culture that needs to shift,

00:45:14.510 --> 00:45:15.830
there's the way that veterans

00:45:15.830 --> 00:45:17.830
can just continue to counsel veterans,

00:45:17.830 --> 00:45:18.730
as Tony has talked about.

00:45:18.730 --> 00:45:21.477
But if you also just share
what your thoughts are

00:45:21.477 --> 00:45:24.130
and your goals in terms
of moving things forward

00:45:24.130 --> 00:45:27.630
so we don't just kind of get
stuck in this cycle and not to,

00:45:27.630 --> 00:45:29.600
I mean, that is a thing that
we've talked about already,

00:45:29.600 --> 00:45:32.180
but even in this cycle
of some of the challenges

00:45:32.180 --> 00:45:35.460
that already exist in the
military and for veterans.

00:45:35.460 --> 00:45:37.420
So Barbara, maybe if you
wanna lead off with that.

00:45:37.420 --> 00:45:38.640
- Sure, and I mean,

00:45:38.640 --> 00:45:40.820
and there's so many ways
we need to move forward.

00:45:40.820 --> 00:45:42.850
So what you're talking about, Steven,

00:45:42.850 --> 00:45:47.780
a lot of the stigma and
trying to let people know

00:45:47.780 --> 00:45:49.000
it's not a weakness.

00:45:49.000 --> 00:45:51.640
I mean, we see it as a form of resilience

00:45:51.640 --> 00:45:56.150
to go get treatment and to work
on it and to, so, you know,

00:45:56.150 --> 00:45:59.020
trying to deal with the stigma.

00:45:59.020 --> 00:46:01.650
Obviously I'm working on treatments,

00:46:01.650 --> 00:46:04.560
not every treatment works
for everybody, for sure.

00:46:04.560 --> 00:46:07.260
And so trying to figure
out new treatments,

00:46:07.260 --> 00:46:11.010
trying to figure out so
personalized medicine

00:46:11.010 --> 00:46:12.310
and biomarkers and all of that,

00:46:12.310 --> 00:46:15.200
to try to figure out
what's gonna work for whom.

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:18.790
And we're trying to come
up with, so for example,

00:46:18.790 --> 00:46:21.470
we do a lot of exposure
therapy and it works very well,

00:46:21.470 --> 00:46:22.650
but not for everyone.

00:46:22.650 --> 00:46:25.400
So we're working on
augmentation approaches,

00:46:25.400 --> 00:46:27.490
trying to identify early on.

00:46:27.490 --> 00:46:29.940
I mean, we can pretty much figure

00:46:29.940 --> 00:46:32.090
by Friday of the first week,

00:46:32.090 --> 00:46:33.890
we can tell if someone
is on the trajectory

00:46:33.890 --> 00:46:35.650
to get the full response or not.

00:46:35.650 --> 00:46:38.410
So we're doing some different
things in the second week

00:46:38.410 --> 00:46:40.130
to try to boost that response.

00:46:40.130 --> 00:46:43.020
So I'm gonna be a lot
on the treatment front,

00:46:43.020 --> 00:46:45.514
but I think we need to do it
all because our treatments

00:46:45.514 --> 00:46:47.620
may not the very best
treatments in the world.

00:46:47.620 --> 00:46:51.340
And if people won't come
forward and say, "I need help,

00:46:51.340 --> 00:46:54.350
can you help me?" then they're
not gonna to help anyone.

00:46:54.350 --> 00:46:56.220
So we gotta work on all of it.

00:46:56.220 --> 00:46:57.523
- Yeah, really well said.

00:46:58.400 --> 00:46:59.520
Steven, maybe to follow up

00:46:59.520 --> 00:47:00.870
on what you already started to allude to,

00:47:00.870 --> 00:47:01.980
but anything that you'd wanna add

00:47:01.980 --> 00:47:04.830
in terms of how we move things forward?

00:47:04.830 --> 00:47:06.960
- Yeah, I mean, I think
it's a multifaceted,

00:47:06.960 --> 00:47:08.830
I think there's not,

00:47:08.830 --> 00:47:13.020
you know, certainly not
one single objective

00:47:13.020 --> 00:47:15.180
or one single initiative.

00:47:15.180 --> 00:47:17.080
I think it's, I mean, it's, that's for me,

00:47:17.080 --> 00:47:18.530
these conversations are super encouraging

00:47:18.530 --> 00:47:20.250
because it pulls me out
of my own little bubble

00:47:20.250 --> 00:47:22.610
and just reminds me
that there's, you know,

00:47:22.610 --> 00:47:24.710
for all the things wrong with our systems.

00:47:26.979 --> 00:47:28.570
I have a system to even complain about

00:47:28.570 --> 00:47:31.170
which wasn't in place 30 years ago, right?

00:47:31.170 --> 00:47:34.420
And it's not in place for
civilians in many cases.

00:47:34.420 --> 00:47:37.680
So that was a realization
I had some time ago

00:47:39.130 --> 00:47:41.200
where it was, you know,

00:47:41.200 --> 00:47:44.130
whatever administrative friction there was

00:47:44.130 --> 00:47:46.290
and seeing a counselor,
doing whatever it was,

00:47:46.290 --> 00:47:47.400
it's just like, you know what?

00:47:47.400 --> 00:47:49.460
There's a lot of people who've
probably seen more violence

00:47:49.460 --> 00:47:51.150
in America than you saw in Afghanistan

00:47:51.150 --> 00:47:53.907
who don't even have a dysfunctional
bureaucracy to turn to.

00:47:53.907 --> 00:47:58.570
And so first off, to the extent
you can count your blessings

00:47:58.570 --> 00:48:02.210
and then, depending on where
you're at in the world,

00:48:02.210 --> 00:48:03.050
what can you do about it?

00:48:03.050 --> 00:48:06.160
And I think the piece
that we're focused on

00:48:06.160 --> 00:48:07.150
that we feel like, you know,

00:48:07.150 --> 00:48:12.010
works to engage in, is
really that policy reform

00:48:12.010 --> 00:48:14.920
within the active duty component.

00:48:14.920 --> 00:48:18.970
In terms of how do we normalize the fact

00:48:18.970 --> 00:48:22.680
that war is not good for the soul

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:24.040
and that there's unseen wounds

00:48:24.040 --> 00:48:25.500
that the sooner we address them

00:48:25.500 --> 00:48:28.860
and not just force people to
stuff them for in some cases,

00:48:28.860 --> 00:48:30.810
20 years, so that they
don't miss promotion

00:48:30.810 --> 00:48:31.930
or they don't miss deployment,

00:48:31.930 --> 00:48:33.463
and then have to deal with it,

00:48:34.730 --> 00:48:37.920
that's criminal, in my
mind, and it's stupid.

00:48:37.920 --> 00:48:42.920
And so that's a lot of what our advocacy

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:46.730
in conjunction with storytelling is,

00:48:46.730 --> 00:48:50.920
is to try and move the
needle from a policy level,

00:48:50.920 --> 00:48:51.967
and within the DOD,

00:48:53.140 --> 00:48:55.990
to stop weaponizing stigma,

00:48:55.990 --> 00:48:58.990
to change policies and change culture

00:48:58.990 --> 00:49:02.350
that can actually be influential
at the point of woundedness

00:49:02.350 --> 00:49:04.373
and not years after the fact.

00:49:07.215 --> 00:49:09.080
There was a film made.

00:49:09.080 --> 00:49:11.130
I think one of the most
instructive things, culturally,

00:49:11.130 --> 00:49:14.870
is there was a film made, that
some of you may be aware of,

00:49:14.870 --> 00:49:17.940
by John Huston, a World War II,
called "Let There be Light".

00:49:17.940 --> 00:49:20.690
And you can watch that
film, I think, on Netflix,

00:49:20.690 --> 00:49:22.390
I think it's still on the platform,

00:49:22.390 --> 00:49:26.970
but in that film, he
tells the story of this,

00:49:26.970 --> 00:49:29.040
essentially a field
hospital that was set up,

00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:30.800
I think it was in Long Island,

00:49:30.800 --> 00:49:33.760
for folks coming back from
the Pacific and Europe

00:49:33.760 --> 00:49:38.470
who were experiencing acute
trauma from their experiences.

00:49:38.470 --> 00:49:41.520
And you watch, in 50
minutes, in black and white,

00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:46.520
you watch these folks have
those wounds acknowledged

00:49:47.860 --> 00:49:52.310
and you see what,

00:49:52.310 --> 00:49:54.690
how they change in two or three weeks

00:49:54.690 --> 00:49:57.090
before we send them back to their home.

00:49:57.090 --> 00:49:59.490
And it's powerful.

00:49:59.490 --> 00:50:01.410
And it's not to say that those are the,

00:50:01.410 --> 00:50:04.750
those modalities to
treatment or maybe crude,

00:50:04.750 --> 00:50:08.120
but just having those wounds
acknowledged within a cohort,

00:50:08.120 --> 00:50:10.270
like you talked about,
Tony, within community,

00:50:10.270 --> 00:50:13.000
before just being unleashed
back in the civilian in world,

00:50:13.000 --> 00:50:14.700
is so essential.

00:50:14.700 --> 00:50:16.740
The night that that
film was to be screened,

00:50:16.740 --> 00:50:18.180
the DOD paid for it.

00:50:18.180 --> 00:50:19.480
The night that that
film was to be screened

00:50:19.480 --> 00:50:21.620
at the Museum of Modern Art in New York,

00:50:21.620 --> 00:50:23.610
it was confiscated by MPs,

00:50:23.610 --> 00:50:26.200
and it was classified until
the Carter administration.

00:50:26.200 --> 00:50:27.033
- Wow.

00:50:27.033 --> 00:50:29.830
- Nobody saw it until it was declassified.

00:50:29.830 --> 00:50:34.460
And that film, which was
honest, and it was hope-filled,

00:50:34.460 --> 00:50:39.460
was replaced by another DOD
film called "Shades of Gray",

00:50:39.950 --> 00:50:43.180
which essentially, the message was,

00:50:43.180 --> 00:50:45.570
if you're a good red blooded American,

00:50:45.570 --> 00:50:47.450
you really shouldn't have a problem

00:50:47.450 --> 00:50:49.293
meeting the communist hoard dead on.

00:50:50.530 --> 00:50:52.400
It's very difficult to watch.

00:50:52.400 --> 00:50:56.560
It's pandering, it's ignorant,
it's insulting, frankly,

00:50:56.560 --> 00:50:58.320
it's almost cartoonish.

00:50:58.320 --> 00:51:02.970
But all that to say is, whether
that was explicit or not.

00:51:02.970 --> 00:51:05.270
That was a film that was
made out of a lot of fear

00:51:05.270 --> 00:51:09.940
based on the next threat that
we saw towards our nation,

00:51:09.940 --> 00:51:13.420
and that idea permeates.

00:51:13.420 --> 00:51:16.170
So for us, it's, you know,

00:51:16.170 --> 00:51:19.560
you don't fix that with
one movie or one book

00:51:19.560 --> 00:51:22.920
or one conversation, but how do you engage

00:51:22.920 --> 00:51:26.980
to shift that narrative back
to something that maybe is hard

00:51:26.980 --> 00:51:29.090
to look at look at, both at an individual

00:51:29.090 --> 00:51:31.608
and a societal level because
there's a lot of failure,

00:51:31.608 --> 00:51:35.490
there's a lot of regret, but
there's also a lot of hope.

00:51:35.490 --> 00:51:39.870
And so really that's where
we feel like we can engage,

00:51:39.870 --> 00:51:41.560
is in that storytelling dynamic

00:51:41.560 --> 00:51:45.140
and hopefully moving the needle
with national policy change,

00:51:45.140 --> 00:51:47.780
knowing that if all of our hope

00:51:47.780 --> 00:51:51.290
is based on Congress changing
policy, we're in bad shape.

00:51:51.290 --> 00:51:56.290
And so the work of Dr.
Rothbaum and Tony and others

00:51:56.960 --> 00:51:59.980
in the community, day in day
out, is absolutely essential.

00:51:59.980 --> 00:52:01.660
Like that's so encouraging

00:52:01.660 --> 00:52:04.320
because that doesn't take
Congress getting its act together.

00:52:04.320 --> 00:52:06.600
We can (indistinct) right
now, in our community,

00:52:06.600 --> 00:52:08.343
so that's super encouraging to me.

00:52:09.660 --> 00:52:10.580
- Thanks so much for sharing all that.

00:52:10.580 --> 00:52:11.413
I mean that's, well,

00:52:11.413 --> 00:52:13.860
that story that you shared
about the films that's,

00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:17.490
well, I'm still, I mean,
just the impacts of that.

00:52:17.490 --> 00:52:20.180
It also makes me think that we
need to do more to highlight

00:52:20.180 --> 00:52:22.230
what Dr. Rothbaum is doing as well.

00:52:22.230 --> 00:52:23.740
If we can summarize, as you
mentioned the beginning,

00:52:23.740 --> 00:52:24.573
'cause I mean,

00:52:24.573 --> 00:52:26.580
that sounds like it was
also part of that first film

00:52:26.580 --> 00:52:29.420
that was just not let out
and just the power of that,

00:52:29.420 --> 00:52:31.760
and Dr. Rothbaum, you talked
about from the effectiveness.

00:52:31.760 --> 00:52:34.477
So Steven, thanks for pulling
out those those nuances

00:52:34.477 --> 00:52:38.660
and just being frank about the
realities in a lot of ways.

00:52:38.660 --> 00:52:41.470
- Yeah, but also highlighting
a message of hope,

00:52:41.470 --> 00:52:43.847
'cause I do think that that's
what we need to put out there,

00:52:43.847 --> 00:52:45.293
is a message of hope.

00:52:47.501 --> 00:52:49.340
- A 100% agreed.

00:52:49.340 --> 00:52:50.640
Tony, you get the last word.

00:52:50.640 --> 00:52:55.363
- So being a military guy
and prior NCO, it's not,

00:52:56.973 --> 00:52:58.428
let's don't talk about, let's be about it.

00:52:58.428 --> 00:53:01.170
And the thing is I want, as far as policy,

00:53:01.170 --> 00:53:03.660
I wanna replicate what the DOD has

00:53:03.660 --> 00:53:04.900
and put it on the veterans' world.

00:53:04.900 --> 00:53:06.037
So that includes families,

00:53:06.037 --> 00:53:08.080
so Why I have this
family thing going, okay?

00:53:08.080 --> 00:53:09.230
That includes family.

00:53:09.230 --> 00:53:10.839
We gotta take what worked in the DOD world

00:53:10.839 --> 00:53:14.680
and replicate it, and it's
gonna take grassroots, okay?

00:53:14.680 --> 00:53:16.040
So that's fine with me.

00:53:16.040 --> 00:53:17.560
If I gotta start it, fine.

00:53:17.560 --> 00:53:19.840
But I think we gotta start
looking at not just a veteran

00:53:19.840 --> 00:53:21.158
as being the person you look at,

00:53:21.158 --> 00:53:21.991
but look at the family as well,

00:53:21.991 --> 00:53:24.770
because sometimes you want
gun safety go to the spouse

00:53:24.770 --> 00:53:27.270
and they'll be more
likely accept gun safety

00:53:27.270 --> 00:53:28.230
than the veteran, all right?

00:53:28.230 --> 00:53:29.284
But we just stuck on the veteran

00:53:29.284 --> 00:53:30.680
and we trying to convince him that,

00:53:30.680 --> 00:53:32.720
oh no, it's good for you.

00:53:32.720 --> 00:53:34.740
So policy on that, that
I keep talking about.

00:53:34.740 --> 00:53:38.270
Also about the locality,
being local, local, local.

00:53:38.270 --> 00:53:41.580
We cannot nationalize,
we cannot monopolize,

00:53:41.580 --> 00:53:43.890
cannot franchise this, okay?

00:53:43.890 --> 00:53:45.870
You gotta start with the local community

00:53:45.870 --> 00:53:47.570
and that's where your funding has to be.

00:53:47.570 --> 00:53:49.540
Okay, so the funding just
can't come from one source

00:53:49.540 --> 00:53:50.373
and then you try it,

00:53:50.373 --> 00:53:51.206
and then you have like one

00:53:51.206 --> 00:53:53.710
or two people covering the
whole state of Florida,

00:53:53.710 --> 00:53:55.470
to try to do some kinda outreach.

00:53:55.470 --> 00:53:57.430
You gotta start bringing in the community,

00:53:57.430 --> 00:53:58.830
'cause the community is them.

00:53:58.830 --> 00:53:59.700
We are no longer,

00:53:59.700 --> 00:54:01.010
people don't understand
that we're veteran,

00:54:01.010 --> 00:54:02.750
we're no longer federalized.

00:54:02.750 --> 00:54:04.940
So when you have, when you
try to do something to me,

00:54:04.940 --> 00:54:06.170
you better make sure you do the same thing

00:54:06.170 --> 00:54:08.160
to anybody else who's American citizen.

00:54:08.160 --> 00:54:10.420
And when I'm a DOD,
I'm a federal employee,

00:54:10.420 --> 00:54:12.100
but as a veteran, I'm an individual.

00:54:12.100 --> 00:54:14.040
So if you're not gonna
take their guns away,

00:54:14.040 --> 00:54:15.360
you're not gonna take my guns away.

00:54:15.360 --> 00:54:16.300
And I'm just saying what I hear.

00:54:16.300 --> 00:54:17.830
I'm not saying that, I'm just saying,

00:54:17.830 --> 00:54:20.610
don't say, I am not a
federal piece of property,

00:54:20.610 --> 00:54:22.010
I'm a constituent.

00:54:22.010 --> 00:54:25.130
So treat us like constituents
of your community,

00:54:25.130 --> 00:54:28.330
that means the county,
the state, the city,

00:54:28.330 --> 00:54:30.600
and the state and federal
all have to play a part

00:54:30.600 --> 00:54:33.160
in taking care of me, not
just the federal government.

00:54:33.160 --> 00:54:34.290
So those are the things that I,

00:54:34.290 --> 00:54:35.980
and when I mentioned with Steven,

00:54:35.980 --> 00:54:38.590
and I agree with that when it
comes to he's a policy guy.

00:54:38.590 --> 00:54:40.220
I think we have a problem
with Congress right now

00:54:40.220 --> 00:54:42.250
because of the two partisan.

00:54:42.250 --> 00:54:43.820
So it's gotta start
here, at the grassroots,

00:54:43.820 --> 00:54:45.690
where everybody's gonna
take care of the veteran.

00:54:45.690 --> 00:54:49.523
And finally, when I see a
battle full of political bodies,

00:54:50.520 --> 00:54:52.640
I don't sit there and start
look at the percentages

00:54:52.640 --> 00:54:54.060
of who's out there, okay?

00:54:54.060 --> 00:54:55.610
I just see a veteran out
there that's laying out,

00:54:55.610 --> 00:54:58.220
needing help, needing care.

00:54:58.220 --> 00:54:59.530
I don't see if it's a female,

00:54:59.530 --> 00:55:00.900
I don't see if it's an African American,

00:55:00.900 --> 00:55:02.870
I just see a veteran out
there that needs help.

00:55:02.870 --> 00:55:05.270
We need to bring that
down to us eventually,

00:55:05.270 --> 00:55:07.370
where we also see a veteran
out there that needs

00:55:07.370 --> 00:55:11.470
versus these little things

00:55:11.470 --> 00:55:12.730
that they put us into that, you know,

00:55:12.730 --> 00:55:13.886
to try to put us in a box.

00:55:13.886 --> 00:55:15.830
So those are my two things, that is,

00:55:15.830 --> 00:55:17.450
spend more money to the community,

00:55:17.450 --> 00:55:20.673
don't forget the families from
kinds of policies, that's me.

00:55:21.590 --> 00:55:24.399
Be about it, don't not just
talk about it, but be about it.

00:55:24.399 --> 00:55:26.060
- Yep, yep.

00:55:26.060 --> 00:55:27.580
Exactly.

00:55:27.580 --> 00:55:28.802
Well, I know we did talk about hoping,

00:55:28.802 --> 00:55:29.900
for me, just having this conversation

00:55:29.900 --> 00:55:31.430
gives me a hope as well.

00:55:31.430 --> 00:55:33.900
I mean, in multiple ways, just
how frank you all have been

00:55:33.900 --> 00:55:34.740
about the challenges,

00:55:34.740 --> 00:55:37.020
but then also the ways that
things have moved forward

00:55:37.020 --> 00:55:39.200
and the ways that we can
continue to move things forward.

00:55:39.200 --> 00:55:42.000
And it's also, I'm grateful
that even this conversation

00:55:42.000 --> 00:55:43.510
is encouraging to you all,

00:55:43.510 --> 00:55:45.010
as you're seeing different pockets

00:55:45.010 --> 00:55:46.820
of the work that's being done.

00:55:46.820 --> 00:55:48.690
